<Hub pillarId="privacy-and-data">
  <p>Your chat is not a diary with a lock you imagined. The company pages say what happens to it, and the switch is yours.</p>
  <h2>Can a chat be used to train a model?</h2>
  <p>
    For ChatGPT, OpenAI’s own help pages are the source, and they differ by plan. On personal Free, Plus, and Pro accounts, <a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/7730893-data-controls-in-chatgpt">data controls</a> say “Improve the model for everyone” is on by default. Turning it off keeps your chat history and stops new conversations from being used to improve the models. OpenAI says content from ChatGPT Business, Enterprise, and Edu workspaces is not used to train its models by default.
  </p>
  <p>
    A temporary chat, OpenAI says, is not used to improve the models and may be kept for up to 30 days for safety. Turning the training setting off does not delete saved chats. The details are in <a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/5722486-how-your-data-is-used-to-improve-model-performance">how your data is used to improve model performance</a>.
  </p>
  <h2>What should stay out of the box?</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="https://www.ncsc.gov.uk/collection/guidelines-secure-ai-system-development">UK National Cyber Security Centre</a> tells people who build AI systems that those systems should work without revealing sensitive data to unauthorised parties. That is a builder’s rule. The matching habit for a user is simple: do not paste passwords, account numbers, medical records, or someone else’s private messages into a chat. <a href="https://openai.com/index/how-chatgpt-protects-privacy/">OpenAI’s own privacy note</a> says not to share sensitive information you would not want used or reviewed.
  </p>
  <p>
    NIST’s Generative AI Profile lists data privacy among the risks of generative systems: leakage or unauthorised use of biometric, health, location, or other sensitive data. The profile is <a href="https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.600-1">NIST AI 600-1</a>.
  </p>
  <p>A full guide for this topic is still being written. The assistant guide already covers the first settings: <a href="/articles/how-to-use-an-ai-assistant/">How to use an AI assistant safely</a>.</p>
</Hub>
